Subject: report a track or notify script that could not be executed
Date: 2026-09-12
Applies-to: keepalived 2.4.3

keepalived forks a child per track or notify script, redirects that child's
stdin, stdout and stderr to /dev/null, and then calls execve. When execve
fails it reports the failure with log_message(), which writes to stderr and to
syslog. In a container neither survives: stderr is the fd that was just
redirected, and syslog needs a /dev/log that is not there. So a script that
cannot be executed produces no output at all, at any log level, and the whole
failure has no observable symptom.

A track script is the worse half. The child exits 0 after the failed execve,
which keepalived reads as the script succeeding, so the node keeps its VIP
through an outage of the service the script was installed to watch. A notify
script's side effect simply never happens.

The two cases that reach this in practice both pass every check keepalived
makes at parse time, so nothing earlier reports them: a script whose shebang
names an interpreter the image does not ship, and a script mounted from a
filesystem carrying noexec.

This keeps a copy of the console across the redirect and reports the exec
diagnostics through it. Nothing else changes: the script's own output still
goes to /dev/null, so no script can write into the log stream the alert rules
in alerts/logql.yaml read.

Drop this patch once a pinned release reports the failure itself.

--- a/lib/notify.c
+++ b/lib/notify.c
@@ -83,6 +83,26 @@
 	use_symlinks = state;
 }
 
+/* A copy of the log console, taken before the child's stderr is redirected to
+ * /dev/null below so that the exec diagnostic in system_call_script() can
+ * still be reported through it. That diagnostic goes out through
+ * log_message(), which under --dont-fork --log-console writes to stderr and to
+ * syslog, and a container has no /dev/log for the syslog leg to reach. So
+ * without this the one report that a track or notify script could not be
+ * executed is written to /dev/null. Upstream keepalived 2.4.3 has no fix. */
+static int console_fd = -1;
+
+static void
+restore_log_console(void)
+{
+	if (console_fd < 0)
+		return;
+
+	dup2(console_fd, STDERR_FILENO);
+	close(console_fd);
+	console_fd = -1;
+}
+
 static bool
 set_script_env(uid_t uid, gid_t gid)
 {
@@ -108,6 +128,7 @@
 
 	/* Prepare for invoking process/script */
 	signal_handler_script();
+	console_fd = fcntl(STDERR_FILENO, F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, STDERR_FILENO + 1);
 	set_std_fd(false);
 
 	return false;
@@ -272,11 +293,13 @@
 		execve(script->path ? script->path : script->args[0], args.execve_args, environ);
 
 		/* error */
+		restore_log_console();
 		log_message(LOG_ALERT, "Error exec-ing command '%s', error %d: %m", script->path ? script->path : script->args[0], errno);
 	} else {
 		retval = system(str = cmd_str(script));
 
 		if (retval == -1) {
+			restore_log_console();
 			log_message(LOG_ALERT, "Error exec-ing command: %s", str);
 			exit(0);
 		}
@@ -284,10 +307,12 @@
 		if (WIFEXITED(retval)) {
 			if (WEXITSTATUS(retval) == 127) {
 				/* couldn't find command */
+				restore_log_console();
 				log_message(LOG_ALERT, "Couldn't find command: %s", str);
 			}
 			else if (WEXITSTATUS(retval) == 126) {
 				/* couldn't find command */
+				restore_log_console();
 				log_message(LOG_ALERT, "Couldn't execute command: %s", str);
 			}
 			else
